<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Electorate of Bavaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1623 CE – 1806 CE · capital Munich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bavaria as one of the great electoral principalities of the Empire, a Catholic power whose dukes twice sought the imperial crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-elector-of-bavaria">Duke Maximilian</a> I, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>, helped crush the Bohemian revolt at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">White Mountain</a> in 1620. As a reward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Ferdinand II</a> transferred the electoral dignity of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> to him in 1623 and gave him the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a> in 1628, gains confirmed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The electorate was firmly Catholic, with strong ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and a rich baroque church culture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphenburg-palace">Nymphenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleissheim-palace">Schleissheim</a> palaces in and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-emanuel-elector-of-bavaria">Max Emanuel</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> and lost his lands after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blenheim">Blenheim</a> in 1704, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Charles Albert</a> was elected Emperor Charles VII in 1742 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">Austrian Succession</a> war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1777 the Bavarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> died out and the electorate passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-theodore-elector-of-bavaria">Charles Theodore</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Elector Maximilian IV Joseph</a> gained large territories and became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> on 1 January 1806.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/electorate-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Electorate of Bavaria" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electoral_Standard_of_Bavaria_(1623-1806).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1623 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maximilian I, Max Emanuel, Charles Albert</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Bavarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kurbayern, Electoral Bavaria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1714&amp;focus=electorate-of-bavaria" aria-label="Electorate of Bavaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Electorate of Bavaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 749L5279 723M5269 749L5229 694M5269 749L5200 712M5269 749L5269 749M5269 749L5281 715M526